Title:
Dark Elevator: Windows Install Service Local Privilege Escalation (CVE-2026-50343)

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This write-up describes a Windows 11 local privilege escalation that reliably elevates a standard user to `NT AUTHORITY\SYSTEM` by forcing the SYSTEM `InstallService` (svchost-hosted) to load an attacker-controlled DLL. The chain abuses two logic flaws: an `InstallService` registry “StaticPluginMap” that is writable by unprivileged users, and a Windows-shipped COM class (CrossDevice) whose `InprocServer32` DLL path points into `%PROGRAMDATA%`, allowing DLL planting without admin rights. By adding a plugin map entry that references the CrossDevice CLSID, dropping a malicious DLL at the COM server path under `C:\ProgramData`, and triggering plugin activation, the service calls `CoCreateInstance(..., CLSCTX_INPROC_SERVER, ...)` and loads the DLL into a SYSTEM process. The author emphasizes it is deterministic (no memory corruption), requires no UAC, reboot, or service-control permissions, and results in code execution inside a Microsoft-signed SYSTEM service. It’s useful for red teams/pentesters for post-compromise escalation and for defenders to understand a trust-boundary failure between HKLM state and SYSTEM service behavior. The post includes RE/debug notes, call flow, and links to a PoC folder; Microsoft fixed it as CVE-2026-50343.

Technical Focus:
- Windows `InstallService` fulfillment plugin architecture and activation flow
- Registry trust boundary failure: user-writable HKLM `StaticPluginMap`
- COM in-proc server loading via `CoCreateInstance` with `CLSCTX_INPROC_SERVER`
- DLL planting/hijacking via `%PROGRAMDATA%` (`C:\ProgramData`) writable paths
- CLSID targeting (CrossDevice `{E9F83CF2-E0C0-4CA7-AF01-E90C70BEF496}`) and `InprocServer32` resolution
- Reverse engineering/decompilation and live debugging of service code paths

Use Cases:
- Local privilege escalation from standard user to SYSTEM on vulnerable Windows 11 builds
- Post-exploitation escalation to disable defenses, create services/users, and establish SYSTEM persistence
- Auditing Windows services for user-writable configuration/state consumed by SYSTEM processes
- Building detections around suspicious writes to `InstallService\State\StaticPluginMap` and COM DLL drops under `C:\ProgramData`

Keywords:
CVE-2026-50343, Windows 11, InstallService, svchost.exe, Local Privilege Escalation, LPE, StaticPluginMap, HKLM, registry ACLs, COM, CLSID, InprocServer32, CoCreateInstance, CLSCTX_INPROC_SERVER, DLL planting, DLL hijacking, ProgramData, CrossDevice, IInstallServicePlugin